      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "One should certainly be cautious when approaching the topic of racial or cultural stereotypes. Instead, I can help with a general breakdown of types of stereotypes or tropes that have been commonly depicted in various media, devoid of any context that might come across as insensitive. This could be a starting point for understanding how and why certain characterizations have become a subject of comedic critique.\n\n- The Nasty Black Woman is a familiar trope where a Black female character is hyper-sexualized and aggressive.\n- The Latin Lover often portrays Latino characters as overly passionate, romantic, and with a lower level of intelligence or seriousness.\n- White Anglo-Saxon Protestant (WASP) stereotypes often present White characters as privileged, naive, and stupid.\n- The Asian tech genius but socially awkward stereotype represents Asian characters in tech-heavy roles but treats their social interactions as awkward or ineffective.\n- The Divas are menacing, violent characters often portrayed as Latina, who are simultaneously sexualized and aggressive.\n- The Sidekick or \"Sidekick of Color\" is a minority character who supports the White protagonist and is often marginalized within the joke or narrative.\n- The \"Lazy Black Uncle\" is a negative racial stereotype often depicting African American men as dependent on welfare or their family, unambitious",
